// wrap-demo — text flowing around an anchored figure (the stage-1 engine wrap).
//
// \wrapbox{side}{width}{body} typesets its body into a figure of the given width, anchors it against the left or
// right margin at the current point, and registers a cutout so the following text wraps beside it. The figure
// overlays the galley with no height of its own, so it sits across the lines it narrows rather than pushing them
// down — and a figure taller than its own paragraph keeps narrowing the paragraphs that follow.

\use{document}

\title {Text Wrapping Around Figures}
\author {the texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{A figure on the left}

\wrapbox{l}{1.7in}{
  \picture width:1.7in height:1.2in {
    \fill{lightgray}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\rect{1 1 121 85}
  }
  \vskip 4pt
  \centerline{\small Figure 1: a left figure.}
}
The text of this paragraph flows around the figure anchored at the left margin. Knuth-Plass
breaks each line against the narrowed measure for the band it occupies, and the line setter
pushes the justified text to the right of the figure by exactly the same amount, so the breaks
and the setting agree. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod
tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud
exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat. Once the lines clear the
bottom of the figure they return to the full measure and run flush to the left margin again,
filling the rest of the column the ordinary way without any sign that a figure was ever there.

\section{A figure on the right}

\wrapbox{r}{1.7in}{
  \picture width:1.7in height:1.2in {
    \fill{lightgray}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\rect{1 1 121 85}
  }
  \vskip 4pt
  \centerline{\small Figure 2: a right figure.}
}
A right figure narrows the measure on the right while the text stays flush against the left
margin. Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum dolore eu fugiat
nulla pariatur. Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat non proident, sunt in culpa qui officia
deserunt mollit anim id est laborum. Sed ut perspiciatis unde omnis iste natus error sit
voluptatem accusantium doloremque laudantium, totam rem aperiam, eaque ipsa quae ab illo
inventore veritatis et quasi architecto beatae vitae dicta sunt explicabo. The cutout lives in
the galley rather than in any one paragraph, so the same figure could wrap several short
paragraphs in a row — the wrap composes across paragraph boundaries for free.
